4:Ta96,[{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Article","headline":"Notary Tax Deductions & the Self-Employment Tax Exemption (2026)","description":"The notarial-fee self-employment tax exemption explained correctly, plus a full breakdown of notary and loan signing agent tax deductions by category.","mainEntityOfPage":"https://thebeardednotary.com/notary-tax-deductions","author":{"@type":"Organization","name":"The Bearded Notary","url":"https://thebeardednotary.com"},"publisher":{"@type":"Organization","name":"The Bearded Notary","url":"https://thebeardednotary.com","logo":{"@type":"ImageObject","url":"https://thebeardednotary.com/icon.png"}},"dateModified":"2026-09-11T00:00:00Z","image":"https://thebeardednotary.com/images/notary-pricing-guide-featured.png"},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://thebeardednotary.com/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Notary Tax Deductions","item":"https://thebeardednotary.com/notary-tax-deductions"}]},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"Are notary fees exempt from self-employment tax?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Only the fee for the notarial act itself (an acknowledgment or jurat) is generally exempt from self-employment tax under federal rules. Travel fees, printing, document handling, administrative charges, and loan signing service fees are not exempt and remain subject to self-employment tax."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Does this exemption depend on whether I call myself a notary or a loan signing agent?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. The exemption applies to the specific fee component for the notarial act, not to a job title. A loan signing agent still gets the exemption on the pure notarial-fee portion of a signing, but the rest of what they charge for handling the signing is not exempt."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the biggest tax deduction for mobile notaries?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Vehicle mileage is typically the largest deduction for mobile notaries, followed by the notarial-fee self-employment tax exemption itself. See our mileage deduction guide for the current IRS rate and record-keeping requirements."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do I need special software to track this?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"You need a way to separate notarial-fee income from everything else you charge (travel, printing, signing service fees), since that split determines what is exempt. Any accounting software that lets you categorize income works — see our accounting software comparison."}}]}]0:{"buildId":"eGPLLUns6oYj9uMFwKJNZ","rsc":["$","$1","c",{"children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"min-h-screen bg-white","children":[["$","header",null,{"className":"border-b-4 border-notary-gold bg-notary-navy text-white","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"mx-auto max-w-5xl px-4 py-6","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-6 flex flex-col gap-4 border-b border-white/10 pb-5 md:flex-row md:items-center md:justify-between","children":[["$","$L2",null,{"href":"/","className":"text-lg font-bold tracking-[0.18em] text-white transition hover:text-notary-gold","children":"THE BEARDED NOTARY"}],["$","$L3",null,{"light":true}]]}],["$","$L2",null,{"href":"/","className":"mb-3 inline-block text-sm font-semibold text-notary-gold transition hover:text-white","children":"Back to Home"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-2 text-sm text-gray-300","children":"Updated September 2026"}],["$","h1",null,{"className":"max-w-4xl text-4xl font-bold md:text-5xl","children":"Notary Tax Deductions & the Self-Employment Tax Exemption"}]]}]}],["$","article",null,{"className":"max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-12","children":[["$","script",null,{"type":"application/ld+json","dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"$4"}}],"$L5"]}]]}],["$L6"],"$L7"]}],"loading":null,"isPartial":false}
5:["$","div",null,{"className":"prose prose-lg max-w-none","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"text-xl text-gray-700 mb-8 font-semibold","children":"There's one tax rule specific to notaries that most general small-business tax content never mentions, plus the full list of deductions that apply to running a notary or loan signing business."}],["$","div",null,{"className":"flex items-center gap-3 text-sm text-gray-600 mb-8","children":[["$","img",null,{"src":"/images/ray-bearded-notary-author-photo.jpg","alt":"Ray, founder of The Bearded Notary","className":"w-10 h-10 rounded-full object-cover border-2 border-notary-gold flex-shrink-0"}],["$","p",null,{"children":["Written by"," ",["$","$L2",null,{"href":"/about","className":"font-semibold text-notary-navy hover:text-notary-gold hover:underline","children":"Ray"}],", former commissioned California Notary Public (2018-2022) — general notarizations and loan signings."]}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"rounded border border-amber-200 bg-amber-50 px-4 py-3 text-sm text-gray-800 mb-8","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Disclosure:"}]," This page includes affiliate links. If you buy or sign up through them, The Bearded Notary may earn a commission at no extra cost to you."]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 p-6 mb-8","children":["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-800","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Disclaimer:"}]," This is general information, not tax advice. Tax rules change and your situation may differ. Confirm anything here against current IRS guidance or with a tax professional before you file."]}]}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"text-3xl font-bold text-notary-navy mt-12 mb-6","children":"The Notarial Fee Exemption (Get This Part Right)"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-700 mb-6","children":"The IRS Taxpayer Advocate Service confirms it directly: fees you earn for performing an official notarial act — an acknowledgment or a jurat — are generally not subject to self-employment tax. That's a real, meaningful exemption. But it's narrower than a lot of notary content makes it sound."}],["$","div",null,{"className":"bg-blue-50 border-2 border-blue-500 p-8 mb-8 rounded","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"text-xl font-bold text-notary-navy mb-4","children":"What's actually exempt vs. what isn't"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"space-y-3 text-gray-700","children":[["$","p",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Exempt from self-employment tax:"}]," The specific fee for the notarial act itself (your state's per-signature acknowledgment/jurat fee)"]}],["$","p",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"NOT exempt — still subject to self-employment tax:"}]," Travel fees, printing charges, document handling, administrative fees, and loan signing service fees"]}]]}]]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-700 mb-6","children":"This is a distinction about which fee component you're being paid, not about your job title. Plenty of content frames this as \"notaries don't pay self-employment tax, but loan signing agents do\" — that's not quite right. If you're a loan signing agent, the pure notarial-fee portion of what you charge is still exempt. The travel fee, printing, and the flat rate you charge for handling the signing itself are not. Since loan signing agents typically earn most of their income from that non-exempt portion, the practical effect is similar, but the underlying rule is about the fee type, not the title."}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-700 mb-8","children":"You still have to report notarial fees as income — they're just excluded from the self-employment tax calculation specifically. This gets reported on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business), with Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax) being where the exemption is actually applied. The IRS's own guidance points to Publication 334 and Tax Topic 554 for the details, and recommends reviewing the Schedule SE instructions carefully if notarial fees are your only Schedule C income."}],"$L8","$L9","$La","$Lb","$Lc","$Ld","$Le","$Lf","$L10","$L11","$L12","$L13","$L14","$L15","$L16","$L17","$L18","$L19","$L1a","$L1b"]}]
